  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 233 PM EDT Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Surface high pressure is shifting off to the \neast, and return southerly flow has developed. Winds will \nincrease this afternoon and gusts in the 10-20 mph range are \nexpected heading into the evening. A dying cold front will push \ndown from the north overnight and bring the chance of a couple \nshowers. However, weak forcing and dry air in the low levels \nshould cause most of this to fall as virga, though a few \nsprinkles are expected in northern areas. Dry and warm weather \nwill return for Wednesday with highs in the 70s to around 80. An\narea of rain will attempt to move into the region on Thursday \ninto Thursday night as isentropic lift occurs on a warm front. \nEPS ensemble members generally favor a wetter solution and a \nfarther north front, while GEFS and CMCE members generally keep \nthe rain to the south. Models have shifted into a bit more of an\nagreement where it looks like at least southern Vermont should \nsee a few showers, but the northward extent remains uncertain. \nHowever, any precipitation would be light and stratiform, with \nthe wet EPS probabilities of a half inch of rain generally \nranging between 20-30 percent.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Following a washed out cold front by the end of the work \nweek, trends have continued to move towards the ECMWF solution \nkeeping most, if not all, precipitation south of the area Saturday \nnight and Sunday. The baroclinic zone responsible for the area of \npotential showers will be draped more over central and southern New \nYork with better forcing southwest of our region. That said, model \nsolutions Sunday afternoon into Monday denote a subtle redevelopment \nof a surface low off the southern New England coast which could \nbring some light shower chances to southern Vermont. All in all, our \nshower chances this weekend are trending lower. Our flow pattern \nwill remain confluent into next week with zonal flow aloft with \ngenerally dry conditions towards next week. Model solutions begin to \nvary by early next week in terms of precipitation chances. An upper \nlevel high over the Great Lakes will set up a dry corridor from the \nGreat Lakes into northern New England, while a moisture axis over \nthe Mid Atlantic attempts to push north. Model uncertainty in how \nmuch dry air there is will determine if moisture and associated \nshower potential are able to penetrate northward into our region, or \nif dry subsident air keeps our shower chances shunted to the south. \nRegardless, zonal northwesterly flow will keep our temperatures \nthrough the weekend into early next week on the seasonably cool side \nwith highs fairly consistent in the mid to upper 60s, and lows in \nthe low to mid 40s, with some cold hollows hovering about the upper \n30s. Sky conditions favor mainly cloudy conditions through the \nextended.",
